WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.000
MARK: So we're going to
talk about some doughs here.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:09.470
One of which is a--

00:00:09.470 --> 00:00:10.700
SPEAKER 2: Before we do
it, I should show them

00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:12.410
how a mathematician
thinks about elasticity.

00:00:12.410 --> 00:00:12.910
MARK: Yes.

00:00:16.379 --> 00:00:18.367
[APPLAUSE]

00:00:18.367 --> 00:00:20.470
SPEAKER 2: Has anyone made bread?

00:00:20.470 --> 00:00:22.760
You should all go home and make bread.

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:26.900
So if you make bread, so there's this
molecule in wheat, which is gluten.

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:28.220
Actually it's two molecules.

00:00:28.220 --> 00:00:30.880
It's glutenin and gliadin.

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:34.475
And basically these are proteins
that basically form cross-links

00:00:34.475 --> 00:00:35.640
with each other.

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:37.980
They stick to each other to form a gel.

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:40.010
And then what happens
is when you need them,

00:00:40.010 --> 00:00:41.900
they tend to line up the molecules.

00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:45.070
And the molecules, they're very long,
and they like to stick to each other.

00:00:45.070 --> 00:00:47.360
And by short range
interactions, and that

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:49.290
basically gives bread
dough its strength.

00:00:49.602 --> 00:00:52.310
SPEAKER 2: Anyway, so if any of
you have baked, what you'll learn

00:00:52.310 --> 00:00:56.210
is that if you want to make the dough
stronger, then you either add salt

00:00:56.210 --> 00:00:59.357
or you use high protein flour,
which gives it more gluten.

00:00:59.357 --> 00:01:01.190
And there are also ways
of making it weaker.

00:01:01.190 --> 00:01:04.760
You can add fats or oil,
sugar or add acidity.

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:07.580
And I think Mark and our
friends at Johnson and Wales

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:10.860
have made for us three
types of dough, right Mark?

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:14.900
MARK: Yeah, courtesy of the baking
department at Johnson and Wales,

00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:19.100
we have this one, this is made,
they're all the same recipe.

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:22.490
One is made with cup for cup,
which is a gluten-free flour,

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:27.290
also developed by a graduate
of Johnson and Wales.

00:01:27.290 --> 00:01:31.250
This is a high protein bread
flour, which is around 30% protein.

00:01:31.250 --> 00:01:37.340
And then this is vital wheat
gluten, which is used often

00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:39.320
in the processed food industry.

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:41.810
It's 75% gluten.

00:01:41.810 --> 00:01:49.250
This is not why people have celiac
disease, which is an immune deficiency

00:01:49.250 --> 00:01:50.000
disease.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:54.852
But perhaps gluten intolerance could
potentially stem from this stuff.

00:01:54.852 --> 00:01:56.935
SPEAKER 2: But we're gonna,
this stuff is amazing.

00:01:56.935 --> 00:01:58.310
We were playing with this before.

00:01:58.310 --> 00:02:01.310
You guys all have to make this
and go home and play with this.

00:02:01.310 --> 00:02:03.040
That was cool.

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:07.010
MARK: Imagine that in
your lower intestine, huh?

00:02:07.010 --> 00:02:10.632
SPEAKER 2: But now let's try the
bread flour, the normal bread flour.

00:02:10.632 --> 00:02:11.590
This is actually high--

00:02:11.590 --> 00:02:12.980
MARK: So this is like
regular bread dough.

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:14.180
SPEAKER 2: This is regular,
but this is high protein.

00:02:14.180 --> 00:02:15.263
You guys didn't even use--

00:02:15.263 --> 00:02:17.300
MARK: This is made and
set overnight, too, so.

00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:18.030
SPEAKER 2: Oh, that's pretty good.

00:02:18.030 --> 00:02:18.590
MARK: You can go.

00:02:18.590 --> 00:02:20.214
SPEAKER 2: Check that out, that's cool.

00:02:20.214 --> 00:02:20.840
You let go.

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:22.380
Go do that again.

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:23.420
Oh, that's pretty cool.

00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:26.060
OK now, this is the most amazing thing.

00:02:26.060 --> 00:02:29.700
This is the gluten-free flour.

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:31.760
And by all appearances,
it looks the same.

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:33.780
Do you see?

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:35.360
Doesn't it look like flour?

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:36.577
MARK: Allowed to develop.

00:02:36.577 --> 00:02:37.660
SPEAKER 2: It's developed?

00:02:37.660 --> 00:02:38.180
MARK: Overnight.

00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:39.054
SPEAKER 2: Overnight.

00:02:39.054 --> 00:02:40.872
Even longer than the regular flour.

00:02:40.872 --> 00:02:41.830
OK, so should we do it?

00:02:41.830 --> 00:02:42.410
MARK: OK, ready?

00:02:42.410 --> 00:02:44.405
SPEAKER 2: Ready, you guys
have to watch this fast.

00:02:44.405 --> 00:02:44.904
Ready?

00:02:44.904 --> 00:02:49.686
MARK: One, two, oh my, God.

00:02:49.686 --> 00:02:51.185
It's like, it's like ricotta cheese.

00:02:51.185 --> 00:02:51.710
SPEAKER 2: This is crap.

00:02:51.710 --> 00:02:52.302
It's like--

00:02:52.302 --> 00:02:54.010
MARK: How do you make
anything from this?

00:02:54.010 --> 00:02:55.718
SPEAKER 2: Do you make
pasta out of this?

00:02:59.837 --> 00:03:00.920
MARK: What was I thinking?

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:02.820
SPEAKER 2: How do you do that?

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:06.020
MARK: Yeah, I mean it's a
challenge, as you can imagine.

00:03:06.020 --> 00:03:08.480
SPEAKER 2: I mean gluten
is really amazing.

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:10.620
MARK: It's magic, it's magic.

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:15.957
And the amount of ingredients necessary
in this dough to poorly simulate--

00:03:15.957 --> 00:03:19.040
SPEAKER 2: So you might be curious
about what's in the gluten free flours.

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:19.550
MARK:

00:03:19.550 --> 00:03:24.350
SPEAKER 2: So actually Mark gave me the
ingredients in this gluten-free flour

00:03:24.350 --> 00:03:25.790
for you all to admire.

00:03:25.790 --> 00:03:28.970
So it has cornstarch,
which doesn't have gluten.

00:03:28.970 --> 00:03:31.820
It has white rice flour,
brown rice flour, no gluten.

00:03:31.820 --> 00:03:35.090
Milk powder, tapioca flour, no gluten.

00:03:35.090 --> 00:03:36.750
Potato starch, no gluten.

00:03:36.750 --> 00:03:40.407
But then at the end,
there's this ingredient.

00:03:40.407 --> 00:03:41.240
It's a white powder.

00:03:41.240 --> 00:03:42.910
MARK: One that made this
lovely sauce over here.

00:03:42.910 --> 00:03:45.710
SPEAKER 2: It's the white powder
that was used in the lovely sauce.

00:03:45.710 --> 00:03:47.125
It's called xanthan gum.

00:03:47.125 --> 00:03:51.690
And xanthan gum, as you saw in
the sauce, it's sort of crappy.

00:03:51.690 --> 00:03:53.330
MARK: Yeah.

00:03:53.330 --> 00:03:55.480
SPEAKER 2: It does bind
things together a bit,

00:03:55.480 --> 00:03:58.480
but that's basically the only thing
in there that binds things together.

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:06.960
Yep, yep, and that's decidedly the best
version of a gluten-free flour really

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:07.960
available on the market.

00:04:07.960 --> 00:04:11.216
SPEAKER 2: Right, and you
compare that with this stuff.

00:04:11.216 --> 00:04:12.340
And it's just unbelievable.

00:04:12.340 --> 00:04:14.840
I mean this stuff basically has the
property that like air doesn't get out.

00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:15.980
I don't know if you've ever baked bread.

00:04:15.980 --> 00:04:18.563
MARK: This is what makes Wonder
Bread so delicious right here.

00:04:18.563 --> 00:04:19.250
This stuff.

00:04:19.250 --> 00:04:20.958
SPEAKER 2: They put
this in Wonder Bread?

00:04:20.958 --> 00:04:21.589
MARK: Yeah.

00:04:21.589 --> 00:04:23.380
Because they don't want
to, they don't want

00:04:23.380 --> 00:04:27.590
to take the time to allow it
to ferment and rise naturally.

00:04:27.590 --> 00:04:29.340
SPEAKER 2: So they
just put that stuff in.

00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:29.990
MARK: Yeah.

00:04:29.990 --> 00:04:33.140
It's easy, it's science.

